WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.610
Pete: Hey, it's Pete.

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:07.170
I'm in Jackson Hole today and I'm hanging
out with a good friend of mine, Mike Zusman.

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:13.020
And, Mike is the founder of Carve Systems-
a security consultancy.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.950
Mike is a pretty well known security researcher
in his own right.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:23.150
So, we actually rode some powder today- hit
the mountain, and decided why not squeeze

00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:24.530
in a g33ktalk.

00:00:24.530 --> 00:00:27.010
[To Mike] Thanks for sitting down and chatting.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:29.029
Mike: No, happy to be here.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:30.029
Thanks.

00:00:30.029 --> 00:00:34.210
Pete: We have an interesting topic lined up.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:39.560
Mike's going to refresh himself a little bit
as we're talking and actually we even have

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:41.420
a little fire place action.

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:43.329
So, make it a little cozier in here.

00:00:43.329 --> 00:00:44.829
Mike: For the true mountain vibe.

00:00:44.829 --> 00:00:45.829
Pete: Yea, totally.

00:00:45.829 --> 00:00:47.899
Why not do something different right?

00:00:47.899 --> 00:00:54.149
So, we want to talk today about mobile application
security, and what you can do to secure your

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:55.149
mobile apps.

00:00:55.149 --> 00:01:00.289
If you're a developer, how should you be thinking
about security in terms of mobile devices

00:01:00.289 --> 00:01:02.239
and mobile applications?

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:03.359
What are some best practices?

00:01:03.359 --> 00:01:06.230
What are some architectural issues?

00:01:06.230 --> 00:01:11.220
What are the main things that you should be
worried about and concerned about as you're

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:13.690
building and architecting your application?

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:23.050
So, Mike, your background is as a programmer,
as a sometime/one-time hacker, as a-

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.050
Mike: Sure.

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.180
Pete: -security consultant...

00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:27.900
Just fill us in and tell us how you got interested
in mobile security in the first place.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:32.800
Mike: So, a long, long time ago, I was an
application developer myself.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:36.900
I was programming on the Microsoft web technology.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:44.920
So, classic ASP, COM, Visual C++ back in the
day, around- up until about 2004 when a made,

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:51.100
kind of a little shift in my career and I
went to work for a SSLVPN application firewall

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:52.320
vendor.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:58.920
And, that's where I started to focus a little
bit more on breaking applications, and having

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:05.030
been someone who had written both secure and
insecure applications, I took to it pretty

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:08.390
easily.

00:02:08.390 --> 00:02:15.799
Coming out of working for this app firewall
vendor, I went into more of a pure corporate

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:22.599
AppSec role and lasted there a little bit
before I just went full-on into AppSec consulting,

00:02:22.599 --> 00:02:28.680
and being a breaker and someone who helps
fix security vulnerabilities.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:31.909
Pete: And, you've spoken at conferences as
well, right?

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:35.230
Mike: Yea, I've been at BlackHat and DefCon
and CanSecWest...

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:42.279
Pete: What's the most basic thing that a software
engineer should realize when they're approaching

00:02:42.279 --> 00:02:43.799
mobile application security?

00:02:43.799 --> 00:02:49.260
How is mobile app security fundamentally different
than regular web app security, for instance?

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.709
Mike: So, when you're creating a web application,
we kind of have a little bit of a walled garden

00:02:55.709 --> 00:02:56.969
mentality.

00:02:56.969 --> 00:03:01.910
We can put comments in our code and can do
sloppy things because we don't have time to

00:03:01.910 --> 00:03:02.910
do them properly.

00:03:02.910 --> 00:03:08.909
And, they sit on our web server, and as long
as that web server doesn't get hacked, or

00:03:08.909 --> 00:03:12.260
compromised, our dirty secrets are somewhat
safe.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:18.730
But, when you're writing a mobile application,
it's similar to writing off the shelf software

00:03:18.730 --> 00:03:21.879
that's going to be in a retail box, or that
you're going to get on a DVD.

00:03:21.879 --> 00:03:26.150
The software's going to be in someone's hands.

00:03:26.150 --> 00:03:30.529
They're going to have the ability to reverse
engineer it and figure out how you wrote it,

00:03:30.529 --> 00:03:35.342
and see that- OK, maybe you've architected
and implemented it properly, or maybe you

00:03:35.342 --> 00:03:38.849
made some mistakes with regard to security
and privacy.

00:03:38.849 --> 00:03:42.870
Pete: And, not to mention that they have control
of the physical device as well, right?

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:43.870
Mike: Right.

00:03:43.870 --> 00:03:47.779
Pete: Which, physical security is sometimes
an overlooked aspect of computer security

00:03:47.779 --> 00:03:54.989
in general, but most computer security professionals
know that if you get access to the physical

00:03:54.989 --> 00:03:57.939
device, in most scenarios, it's pretty much
game over.

00:03:57.939 --> 00:03:59.019
Mike: Sure.

00:03:59.019 --> 00:04:00.400
We have that same problem with laptops.

00:04:00.400 --> 00:04:03.019
But, with laptops, we have additional controls.

00:04:03.019 --> 00:04:10.550
We have full disk crypto and things like that
and we have access control, we have domain

00:04:10.550 --> 00:04:14.199
policies, and there's a lot of hardening that
can be done to a corporate device or even

00:04:14.199 --> 00:04:15.379
a personal device.

00:04:15.379 --> 00:04:21.810
A personal laptop that if you happen to lose
it, your risk is somewhat controlled.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.630
But, with these mobile devices, they're not
as mature.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:30.680
We don't have a lot of the controls or the
ability to harden or lock down these devices

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:31.880
as we do with a laptop.

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:36.120
So, if writing an application that deals with
potentially sensitive data like credit card

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:43.460
data or even just email, how you store that
data on the device becomes very important.

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:50.910
If that device is lost, or stolen, or even
jail broken now- Now that we're developing

00:04:50.910 --> 00:05:00.340
on these sandbox environments on a mobile
devices, there's sometimes an err of, 'well,

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:05.440
the OS is going to handle this stuff, so we
don't need to handle it as developers.'

00:05:05.440 --> 00:05:11.140
But, once a user jailbreaks their device,
a lot of that security is removed.

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:15.880
So, now your application is in a far more
hostile environment than you original thought

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.110
it or expected it to be.

